WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.440
"The Green Knight" is a fresh take on the classic&nbsp;
hero's journey. Like with so many heroic odysseys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.880
"The Green Knight" is many things,&nbsp;
but, above all else it is this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:14.240
extremely and incontrovertibly weird.
There are magic spells, ghosts, giants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.960
spiritual foxes, and nightmarish fates. What in&nbsp;
this supernatural world is real, and what is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.720
imagining of our hero Sir Gawain? The answer to&nbsp;
that isn't immediately or easily illuminated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.240
Similarly, even the way the story&nbsp;
ends seems almost shrouded in mystery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.280
Not to worry, though -- we can work&nbsp;
the strands of this strange tapestry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.292
together until they weave a pattern that&nbsp;
at least makes some semblance of sense.&nbsp;

00:00:35.292 --> 00:00:39.200
"The Green Knight" is the tale of a young man&nbsp;
named Gawain, who just so happens to be the son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.233
of Morgan le Fay, and, thus, the nephew of&nbsp;
King Arthur and Queen Guinevere.

00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:47.280
 "The Green&nbsp;Knight" never uses these names — other than&nbsp;
Gawain's — but as the tale is based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.000
14th century romantic epic "Sir Gawain and the&nbsp;
Green Knight," we can make some inferences.&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.720
At the start of our tale, Gawain, played by Dev&nbsp;
Patel, leads a free, uneventful life full of wine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.440
women, and song. He seems to spend more nights&nbsp;
in the bed of one woman in particular, Essel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.901
played by Alicia Vikander, who dreams of&nbsp;
being his lady in the courtly royal sense.&nbsp;

00:01:05.901 --> 00:01:10.720
But it's not until Christmas Day that the die is&nbsp;
cast. King Arthur invites Gawain to sit with him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.535
and Guinevere while they discuss the possibility&nbsp;
that Gawain might someday have knightly tales to&nbsp;tell. 

00:01:15.535 --> 00:01:19.520
Gawain's mother summons the titular Green&nbsp;
Knight, who challenges all knights in the hall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.979
to cut him with his own axe, and, in exchange,&nbsp;
have the same wound visited upon them by the&nbsp;Green Knight in a year's time.&nbsp;

00:01:26.163 --> 00:01:27.190
"I'll meet him."&nbsp;

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.601
"You understand this challenge?"

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:30.686
"I do."&nbsp;

00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:35.040
Gawain accepts the challenge and, thinking he's&nbsp;
solved the situation, chops the Green Knight's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.760
head clean off. But the Green Knight is no mere&nbsp;
mortal, so he picks up his own head and rides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.920
away, laughing all the way in the knowledge&nbsp;
that he will kill Gawain in a year's time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.492
This moment is so important because it tells us&nbsp;
what this incarnation of Gawain is all about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.492 --> 00:01:52.800
There are many fictional accounts of Gawain which&nbsp;
portray him at times as noble and at other times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.200
as graceless, callow, and utterly dishonorable.
"The Green Knight" portrays Gawain as a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.160
manipulated too easily by his own ambition. This&nbsp;
Gawain wants to have songs sung of his gallantry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.840
and he wants people to see him as honorable.&nbsp;
However, from the outset, he seems more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.000
interested in perception and power than he does&nbsp;
with the actual responsibilities of knighthood.&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.840
When Gawain arrives, one year later, at the&nbsp;
Green Chapel as the Green Knight demanded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.240
axe in hand, hypothetically ready to be&nbsp;
decapitated, there exists a duality of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.764
desire within him -- one which wishes to die a&nbsp;
hero, and another which wishes to live and become&nbsp;king. 

00:02:23.764 --> 00:02:28.134
The end of "The Green Knight" is about&nbsp;
understanding the consequences of both choices.&nbsp;

00:02:28.134 --> 00:02:33.395
The end of "The Green Knight" is split into two&nbsp;
sections: what could happen and what does happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.395 --> 00:02:37.841
In each, Gawain has in his possession a magic&nbsp;
belt that is meant to protect him from any blow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.841 --> 00:02:41.760
And yet at first, we see Gawain distrust&nbsp;
his own magic and flee -- reflecting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.920
cowardice we see in him throughout the story.
During his quest, Gawain is defeated by three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.240
lowly thieves, we watch him accidentally drug&nbsp;
himself with hallucinogenic mushrooms, and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.880
see him betray the word he gives to a lord, played&nbsp;
by Joel Edgerton, as he keeps the lord's property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.600
and gives himself over sexually to the lord's&nbsp;
wife, who is also played by Alicia Vikander.&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.784
Again and again, Gawain denies his own knighthood&nbsp;
when that title would get him into trouble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.784 --> 00:03:07.600
And yet when Gawain returns home after running&nbsp;
away and forsaking his word to the Green Knight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.034
as well, he still accepts knighthood&nbsp;
from Arthur as though he deserves it.&nbsp;

00:03:11.034 --> 00:03:15.120
Not only that, but Gawain also takes a son&nbsp;
he has with Essel, leaving her forsaken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.560
marries into royal blood, and becomes king -- a&nbsp;
terrible king. Gawain's subjects despise him and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.041
while his magic belt protects him, he watches&nbsp;
his son die and his kingdom fall to ruin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.041 --> 00:03:29.600
In the end, Gawain dies a failure to his name&nbsp;
and his people with neither honor nor nobility.&nbsp;

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.760
And then suddenly, we're back in the Green Chapel&nbsp;
as though nothing ever happened -- Gawain's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.840
future a mere glimpse of things to come.&nbsp;
In the face of that horrifying destiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.000
Gawain removes his protective belt and&nbsp;
allows the Green Knight to lop off his head.&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.400
We get one earlier glimpse of this side of Gawain&nbsp;
when he helps the ghost of Winifred find her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.040
head at the bottom of a lake. In that moment,&nbsp;
Gawain helps Winifred find peace. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.120
"The Green Knight" is about how it's better for&nbsp;
Gawain to die an honorable person than to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:00.560
a long, miserable life, a stranger to oneself,&nbsp;
and a victim of one's own most craven nature.&nbsp;

